# What wakes yeast up? A sugar-and-yeast fair test
#
# Compare three ordinary mixtures on one shared clock. Use comfortably warm,
# not hot, water in a real experiment; ask an adult before using food, do not
# taste lab mixtures, and never tightly seal an active fermentation vessel.
register lv1

# Control 1 has water and sugar but no living culture.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 table_sugar 10g

# Control 2 has water and yeast but no sugar food.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
add v2 dry_yeast 1g

# Test 3 changes only the missing ingredient: it has the same water, sugar and
# yeast doses. The sugar dissolves before the shared clock starts.
new beaker
add v3 water 100mL
add v3 table_sugar 10g
add v3 dry_yeast 1g
inspect v1
inspect v2
inspect v3

# Ten minutes pass for every vessel at once. Only v3 has dissolved sugar and a
# hydrated yeast culture, so only it may emit the computed GasProduced and
# Fermented events. The CO2 bubbles are matter made by the installed balanced
# pathway, not an animation timer.
wait 600s
inspect v1
inspect v2
inspect v3

# Try equal dry-solids fresh yeast, a different temperature or a smaller sugar
# dose. This is a bounded classroom model, not proof that a real mixture is
# safe to drink, bake, bottle or seal; growth, oxygen switching, inhibition,
# flavour compounds and flexible balloons remain explicit future work.

# HOW FAST, AND HOW MUCH OF THAT IS CLAIMED. On 2026-09-16 the yeast rate
# was fitted for the first time: the constant is the one that makes a gram
# of yeast in a litre of this same sugar strength give up 1.5 g of ethanol
# in the first hour at 30 degC, which is the fastest specific rate published
# for anaerobic brewer's and baker's yeast (Pagliardini et al. 2013,
# Microbial Cell Factories 12:29). Before it, ten minutes here took 58.6% of
# the sugar and made about 1.7 litres of carbon dioxide out of a 100 mL
# beaker - more gas than the beaker holds. It now takes about 3.7% and makes
# roughly 105 mL, which is a balloon a class can see.
#
# THAT IS AN UPPER BOUND AND NOT A PREDICTION. The cited rate belongs to a
# laboratory strain growing in a fermenter, not to supermarket yeast in cold
# sugar water; a gram of dry yeast is counted here as a gram of living
# cells, which it is not; and this model's yeast never multiplies, never
# pauses to wake up and is never slowed by the alcohol it makes. One
# published point is matched. The shape of the curve through it is not
# claimed, so a longer wait on this bench runs faster than a real one would,
# and the hydration ramp in front of it is a matter of seconds where a real
# packet of dried yeast wants ten to fifteen minutes in warm water.
